---
name: spec
version: 1
---

# Goal
Someone who has never seen an .intent file can read, write and verify one using only this page.

# Evals
- test: every example on this page parses → intent examples "$INTENT_FILE"
- judge: a developer could write a working parser from this page alone, without guessing
- review: it describes what I mean by .intent

# Fulfillment
## The shape of a file

An .intent file is plain UTF-8 text. It has front matter, then three required sections and one optional one, always in this order:

| Section | What it holds | Locked within a version? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `# Goal` | one plain sentence stating the expected outcome | yes |
| `# Evals` | the checks that say whether the work meets the goal | yes |
| `# Fulfillment` | the work itself: text, design, spec or code | no |
| `# Results` | recorded verdicts for evals (optional) | no |

Section headings use a single `#`. Inside Fulfillment, use `##` and below. A `#` line inside a code block is not a heading.

## Front matter

The file starts with `---`, a few `key: value` lines, and a closing `---`.

- `name`, required: lowercase letters, digits, `.`, `_` or `-`.
- `version`, required: a whole number, 1 or higher.
- `supersedes`, optional: the version this one replaces, written `name@version`.

Other keys are allowed, and tools ignore keys they don't know.

## Goal

One plain sentence. No lists, no second paragraph. The goal says what the work is *for*, not what it contains.

## Evals

Each eval is a list item that starts with its kind:

| Kind | Who runs it | Blocks when it fails? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `test:` | any computer, as a shell command | yes |
| `judge:` | an AI-assisted computer, as a plain-sentence instruction | yes |
| `review:` | a person | yes |
| `watch:` | measured from the outside world, such as traffic or adoption | never |

A `judge:` eval never names an AI provider or model. Whatever assistant the computer has is the judge.

Every eval has a status: **✓** pass, **✗** fail, or **○** pending. Only ✗ blocks. A file can be partly verified: an ordinary computer runs the tests and leaves the judges ○, and that is a valid state, not an error.

## Writing tests

A test is a shell command that passes when it exits with 0. It runs in the folder that holds the file, with the file's path in the `INTENT_FILE` environment variable. Three forms are valid:

- **One line.** The text is the command: `- test: npm run build`
- **Named.** A readable name, an arrow, then the command: `- test: the site builds → npm run build`. Readers see the name. `->` works too.
- **Fenced.** A name, with an indented code block underneath that holds the script.

A `watch:` can use the named or fenced form to measure something; without a command it is a description waiting to be measured.

## Fulfillment

The actual work, written in Markdown. It is free to change at any time, as long as the evals still pass.

## Results

Recorded verdicts live at the bottom of the file, one per line, matched to evals by kind and position:

````text
- judge 1: ✓ 2026-11-12 — the PDF/Markdown contrast lands in the first paragraph
- review 1: ○ pending
````

Tests are not usually recorded here, because they run every time. Recording a verdict never needs a new version.

## The contract

Within a version, the goal and evals never change. To change either one, write a new version: raise `version` by one and set `supersedes` to the old `name@version`. The old version stays readable, so the history shows how the goal evolved, and old evals can be run again on new work to catch regressions.

When an eval fails, the work no longer meets its goal. There are two ways forward: change the fulfillment until it passes, or write a new version with a new goal.

## Not in this spec

- How a tool treats commands in a file from someone it doesn't trust.
- Which AI assistant runs judges.
- How a file looks when rendered.

## A complete example

````intent
---
name: onboarding
version: 2
supersedes: onboarding@1
---

# Goal
New users book their first match within 5 minutes of signup.

# Evals
- test: signup to booking takes under 5 minutes → ./checks/median-signup-to-booking.sh 300
- test: every screen has a back button
  ```sh
  ./checks/screens.sh | grep -v 'back: yes' && exit 1 || exit 0
  ```
- judge: the flow feels effortless for a first-time player
- review: support agrees the copy answers the usual first questions
- watch: weekly share of new users who book within a day

# Fulfillment
## Steps
1. Pick a sport.
2. Pick a nearby club.
3. Pick a time and book.

# Results
- judge 1: ✓ 2026-11-12 — three short steps, no dead ends
- review 1: ○ pending
````
